            %p
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              This is the special case of Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HomotopyToChainHomotopy"}
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi f
                      %mn 0
                      %mo *
                    %mo =
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0^\ast = id
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi f
                      %mn 1
                      %mo *
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1^* = 0
              in positive degree.
          %h3#OverNConnectedDomains
            Over
            = succeed "-connected" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            More generally, the conclusion of the Poincaré lemma for differential forms of bounded degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq n
            follows already on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-connected+topological+spaces"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              \-connected spaces
            (for instance by combining the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+theorem"} Hurewicz theorem
            first with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+coefficient+theorem#UniversalCoefficientTheoremInRationalCohomology"} universal coefficient theorem
            and then with the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+theorem"} de Rham theorem
          %p Explicitly:
          %p
          #ForOneFormsOnSimplyConnectedDomain.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              On a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply-connected+topological+space"} simply-connected
              (i.e.: 1-connected)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+1-form"} differential 1-form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              is exact, with potential function given at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
              by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+of+differential+forms"} integral
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              from any fixed base point along any smooth path to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            This follows locally for instance by the fiberwise Stokes theorem (
            %a{:href => "Stokes+theorem#StokesTheoremForFiberIntegration"}> here
            ) and then globally due to the independence of the choice of path, by the assumption of simple-connectivity and the plain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stokes+theorem"} Stokes theorem
          %p
            Textbook accounts which make this explicit include
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Carmo94"} do Carmo 1994, Prop. 3 (p. 24) in §3
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://unapologetic.wordpress.com/2011/12/17/simply-connected-spaces-and-cohomology/"} Armstrong 2017
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong Poincaré lemma
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+potential"} Kähler potential
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stokes+theorem"} Stokes theorem
            %li
              %p
